XSS discipline (js/dom.js) — one escaper, one element builder, and a rule for which to use where:
```js
export function esc(str) {
  return String(str ?? '').replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ({ '&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;' }[c]));
}
export function el(tag, props = {}, children = []) {
  const node = document.createElement(tag);
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(props)) {
    if (v == null) continue;
    if (k === 'class') node.className = v;
    else if (k === 'dataset') Object.assign(node.dataset, v);
    else if (k.startsWith('on') && typeof v === 'function') node.addEventListener(k.slice(2).toLowerCase(), v);
    else node.setAttribute(k, v);
  }
  for (const c of [].concat(children)) node.append(c && c.nodeType ? c : document.createTextNode(String(c ?? '')));
  return node;
}
```
Rule enforced across every view file: **any node containing a value that originated from Google Places, another user's account, or free-text input (business names, addresses, review text, author names/photos, friend names/emails) is built with `el()`/DOM property assignment, never `innerHTML` string concatenation.** `innerHTML`+template-literal rendering is reserved for fully static or already-escaped chrome (chip lists built from the local `CATEGORY_META` table, star-rating markup where the only interpolated value is a numeric `--fill` percent). Link/URI attributes get an extra allowlist gate before being set: `href` only assigned when the value matches `/^https?:\/\//i` (website, mapsUri, review profileUri), `tel:` hrefs built by stripping to `[0-9+]` first — this blocks `javascript:`/`data:` payloads smuggled through Places or account data from ever reaching an anchor's `href`.
